<Article slug="failure-handling">
<p>Your application receives 1,000 product-page requests each second. Redis answers 95% of them. The database handles the remaining fifty, comfortably within a budget of a hundred such loads per second.</p>
<p>Then the application loses its connection to Redis.</p>
<p>The database still contains every product. The application still knows how to load one. Yet sending every request there would increase its work from fifty loads per second to a thousand: twenty times the normal demand, and ten times the available budget.</p>
<p>Play the failure below. Redis becomes unavailable after two seconds and returns after seven. Watch what happens to the source queue, then replay with bounded admission.</p>
<FailureCapacity />
<p>Without a limit, the model accepts work faster than it can finish. The growing queue represents promises the application has made without enough capacity to fulfil them promptly. Restoring Redis does not remove those old promises. If Redis returns empty, even new requests continue to need the source.</p>
<p>With admission limited to the source budget, the queue disappears—but some callers are refused fallback. That cost is visible. The policy preserves useful service for the requests it can afford, rather than pretending an unavailable cache leaves the system’s capacity unchanged.</p>

<h2>Cache failure changes the capacity contract</h2>
<p>For this simple workload, source demand is the request rate multiplied by the fraction that cannot be answered from cache. At a hit rate of 95%, that fraction is 5%. Losing the cache changes it to 100%, so demand grows by a factor of 100 ÷ 5 = 20.</p>
<p>This is arithmetic about the example, not a Redis performance estimate. It assumes each miss requires one equally expensive load and that no other traffic uses the source. Real product pages may need several queries; one cheap lookup and one expensive aggregation should not consume interchangeable “request” tokens merely because both count as one call.</p>
<p>The queue model is also optimistic: the source keeps completing a hundred loads each second regardless of the backlog. Real overload can reduce useful throughput as requests hold memory, connections and other resources for longer. A capacity model should be checked against the actual query mix and measured resource limits. The illustration establishes why there must be a limit; it does not choose yours.</p>
<p>A cache can therefore be optional for reconstructing data while essential to serving the current traffic volume. “We can always fall back to the database” is incomplete until it specifies how much work the database can accept and what happens to the rest.</p>
<p>The previous article’s coalescing helps when callers need the same answer. Here, imagine the misses need different product cards. One load per key still produces too much aggregate work. A complete loading path needs both duplicate suppression and admission control.</p>

<h2>Distinguish a cache miss from a cache error</h2>
<p>A successful Redis <code>GET</code> can return a value or nil. Nil says the key was absent when the command executed. A connection error or timeout says the application did not obtain a usable result. It does not establish that the key is absent.</p>
<p>You may route both a miss and a cache error towards the same bounded source-loading path. Preserve the distinction in metrics and decisions: a burst of errors is a dependency incident, while misses may be ordinary workload. Treating both as an undifferentiated “not found” also invites a worse mistake: returning an absent product because the source lookup failed. Failure to obtain an answer is not an answer about existence.</p>
<p>Nor are all cache-operation failures equally disposable. If a source read succeeds but saving its result in Redis fails, this request can often return the source answer while recording the failed fill. If a required invalidation fails after a source update, an old copy may remain available. That is the <a href="/learn/databases/caching/invalidation/">invalidation-delivery problem</a>, with a correctness obligation that a catch-and-ignore block does not discharge.</p>
<p>Define outcomes explicitly in the application: cache hit, successful miss, cache unavailable, source answer, source failure and admission refused. This gives the failure path enough information to make a decision without reconstructing meaning from a generic exception.</p>

<h2>Admission control and load shedding</h2>
<p><strong>Admission control</strong> decides whether new work may enter a limited resource. <strong>Load shedding</strong> refuses some work to keep the system within its operating envelope. For cache fallback, the decision belongs before the expensive source operation begins.</p>
<p>A useful implementation can cap active source loads with a semaphore—a fixed set of available permits—and bound any waiting queue. Acquire a permit before starting a load; release it when that operation finishes or is actually cancelled. If the caller merely stops waiting while its query continues, releasing the permit immediately allows another query to start on top of work still consuming capacity.</p>
<p>A concurrency limit adapts naturally to slower operations: the same number of permits remains occupied for longer, so fewer new loads start. It does not guarantee a particular CPU or database budget, especially when query costs differ. A start-rate limit can complement it, but a rate limit alone does not cap the number of long-running operations already in progress.</p>
<p>The queue is part of the budget too. A thousand callers waiting behind ten permits still occupy application resources, and may have no chance of completing before their deadlines. Refuse work that cannot wait usefully. A longer queue stores demand; it does not create execution capacity.</p>
<p>Scope matters. Ten permits in each of fifty application processes allow up to five hundred loads, not ten. Autoscaling the application can expand that total while the database remains the same size. Allocate budgets across the fleet and protect the source itself where possible. Account for other services, writes and background jobs that share it.</p>
<p>The admission mechanism must also survive the failure it is meant to contain. If acquiring a fallback permit requires a command to the same unavailable Redis instance, it cannot be the only protection. Local bounds can provide immediate protection, with their fleet-wide sum understood; a source-side limit can enforce a broader boundary.</p>
<p>Refreshes, retries and warm-up jobs spend source capacity too. Give them an explicit share rather than allowing a second, unmetered loading path to bypass the demand-path limit. During an incident, pausing low-value background work can preserve room for useful foreground requests.</p>

<h2>Timeouts must fit an end-to-end deadline</h2>
<p>A timeout limits a wait. A request deadline limits the time available for the whole request. They are related, but assigning every stage its own generous timeout can make the total far exceed what the caller will tolerate.</p>
<p>Suppose a response must finish within 200 ms. In this illustration, loading from the source needs 80 ms and completing the response needs another 10 ms. Change how long the request spends waiting for Redis, then enable a retry.</p>
<FailureDeadline />
<p>At a 40 ms cache timeout, one failed attempt leaves enough room for the source. Retrying after 20 ms of backoff spends 100 ms on cache handling, leaving just enough room for the model’s 90 ms fallback. Increase the cache timeout to 60 ms: two attempts plus backoff spend 140 ms, and the source budget no longer fits.</p>
<p>The retry can be individually reasonable yet make the overall request impossible. For an optional cache lookup, another attempt may be less useful than reserving time for an already-authorised fallback. If fallback is unavailable too, extending the wait indefinitely still does not create an answer.</p>
<p>Carry the remaining deadline through connection acquisition, cache commands, source admission, source execution and response work. A connection timeout does not necessarily limit a command on an existing connection; an outer request timer does not necessarily empty a client’s internal queue. Inspect the client’s precise behaviour rather than assuming one timeout option covers every wait.</p>
<p>The fixed 80 ms source time makes the budget visible. Real durations vary, so choose budgets from measured distributions and the response objective, then test slow tails. Starting work with a nominally sufficient budget does not guarantee it will finish in time. Propagate cancellation where supported and separately account for work that continues.</p>
<p>The <a href="/learn/databases/caching/examples/failures.py" download>Redis failure worksheet</a> makes that last distinction concrete. With Python 3 and Docker available, run <code>python3 failures.py</code>. It creates a disposable Redis 8.2.2 container with no published ports, pauses command processing, and stops waiting for a child command after 100 ms without cancelling it. The command later completes. A delayed <code>SET</code> still writes its value, confirmed by another read.</p>
<p>The worksheet also checks that a hit, a nil reply and connection refusal produce different outcomes. Its timeout belongs to the Python waiting wrapper, not Redis; Docker overhead is included, so these timings are not performance guidance. It demonstrates why abandoning a wait is insufficient to establish cancellation. Production client abort and reconnection behaviour require their own tests.</p>

<h2>Retry budgets prevent amplification</h2>
<p>A retry makes another attempt in the hope that a transient failure has passed. When the failure is overload, another attempt can instead prolong it. Hidden retries are particularly costly: the application, loader and database client may each believe they are making a modest effort.</p>
<RetryExpansion />
<p>With three application attempts and three loader attempts per call, one user request can generate nine source attempts. Set either layer to one attempt and the multiplication disappears. The figure assumes every attempt fails; an earlier success would stop its retry sequence.</p>
<p>Choose deliberately which layer owns retries. Limit total attempts and elapsed time, and give retries a share of the same admission budget as first attempts. A retry allowance is permission to spend more capacity, not a separate pool of free work.</p>
<p>Backoff increases the delay between attempts. Jitter varies the delay so callers do not all return at the same instant. Both can improve timing, but neither removes the nine leaves in the illustration. Once the retry budget is exhausted, stop.</p>
<p>Also consider whether repeating the operation is safe. A timed-out read may waste work if repeated; a timed-out write may already have changed state. Even repeating a cache fill with the same old value can conflict with an intervening invalidation. Use the operation’s actual idempotency and version rules, not the assumption that a timeout means nothing happened.</p>

<h2>Circuit breakers and cold-cache recovery</h2>
<p>A <strong>circuit breaker</strong> remembers recent failures and temporarily prevents further calls to a dependency. When closed, it allows calls. When open, it fails them promptly or routes them to the application’s fallback policy. After a cooldown, a half-open state allows a limited probe of recovery.</p>
<p>The state belongs to application or client logic; Redis does not automatically apply this policy to your product-page loader. Try the sequence below with failed connections, then finish the recovery probe with a successful miss.</p>
<CacheCircuit />
<p>The first five requests are concurrent. All have already attempted Redis before their failures open the circuit. A threshold does not retroactively prevent calls in flight. The next burst bypasses Redis, and half-open permits only one outstanding probe even if another burst arrives.</p>
<p>A successful nil reply shows that this cache operation completed. It can close the circuit under this policy while leaving the requested answer absent. The source budget remains necessary in closed, open and half-open states. A circuit breaker saves repeated waits and attempts; it does not provide fallback capacity.</p>
<p>The example’s three-failure threshold and three-second cooldown are teaching values. Production policies need appropriate failure classification, observation windows and probe limits, and their state transitions need testing. Per-process breakers also create many probes across a fleet. Stagger recovery attempts and avoid a synchronised rush when a shared timer expires.</p>
<p>Separate three recovery signals: can the application communicate with Redis, does Redis hold useful answers, and does the source have spare capacity? None implies the other two. An empty but responsive cache can keep sending almost all traffic to the source. A warm cache can coexist with a large backlog created during the outage.</p>
<p>In the opening illustration, uncheck <em>Redis returns empty</em> to compare preserved data with cold recovery. Its rising hit rate is a supplied scenario, not a forecast from the model. Real warming depends on request repetition, successful fills, eviction and the work budget. Observe those effects and restore traffic or background loading gradually; do not equate a successful <code>PING</code> with recovered application capacity.</p>

<h2>Graceful degradation needs an explicit answer policy</h2>
<p>When a source load is refused, the application still owes the caller a defined outcome. It might omit optional recommendations, return a cheaper summary, serve an independently available copy within an agreed age bound, or return a controlled unavailable response. These choices preserve different parts of the product experience.</p>
<p>Check that the alternative is actually cheaper and available during the failure. A “fallback” query that scans more data can make the incident worse. An old value stored only in unreachable Redis cannot help. A local copy may help, but creates the coherence and memory responsibilities of another cache tier, covered later in this collection.</p>
<p>Choose permitted degradation per feature. A product description might tolerate an older copy; the authority to complete a purchase may require a current source decision. Never silently turn an unavailable permission check into permission granted, or an unavailable inventory check into guaranteed stock.</p>
<p>Exercise the entire path before an incident: Redis refusing connections, Redis responding slowly, a reachable empty cache, failed fills, source saturation, deadline expiry and repeated recovery. Observe successful user outcomes, response latency, cache errors separately from misses, source attempts and active work, queueing, refusals, retries and served-answer age. A healthy Redis process alone cannot tell you whether the application has recovered.</p>
<p>The governing decision is how much useful work the system can still afford. Preserve that budget through every path into the source, and make the remaining user experience deliberate. The next article examines the <a href="/learn/databases/caching/clients-and-latency/">Redis client queues, connections and round trips</a> inside those waits.</p>

